+# Closure of the independent review's F-1, F-2 and F-3Reviewed package: `evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/`Review: `evidence/usb-descriptor-independent-review-v1/INDEPENDENT-REVIEW-20261121.md`Verdict reviewed: `CONDITIONAL — SPECIFIC FINDINGS REMEDIATION REMAINS`, findings F-1…F-10.This document closes F-1, F-2 and F-3, records two errors in the review's own claims,and states the status of every other finding. Nothing here was accepted: no approval,no merge, no commit, no push, and no device was touched.Repository state when this work was done — recorded before any edit, and unchanged by it:```branch                       arena/c8a76776-pc5HEAD                         56c58521580e88c4339ceb6eb40ce724a2bb533brefs/heads/main (origin)     56c58521580e88c4339ceb6eb40ce724a2bb533brefs/heads/arena/c8a76776-pc5  ae9f3e25f66ac3d31b2b7b64dccef7695fef1d73tracked modifications        11 files (the 2 of the remediation + 9 of this closure)untracked files              234 before this closure, 271 after it was complete                             (`git status --porcelain -uall | grep -c '^??'`; without -uall, 8 entries,                             because git collapses untracked directories)commits / pushes / PR actions 0 / 0 / 0```---## F-1 — reproducibility of `make check` — CLOSED**What the review said (medium).** A green `make check` in a clean clone silentlydepends on `unicorn==2.1.4` being importable; without it 103 tests error out, so theharness is not reproducible from the repository alone. The review also asserted thatthe citation gate additionally requires `refs/pull/*/head`, which no default clonefetches.**The dependency half: confirmed and fixed.** In a clean clone of `main` at`56c5852`, with the pinned module deliberately hidden (`PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1`):```$ python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_*.py'Ran 889 tests in 60.803sFAILED (errors=103, skipped=24)                                    exit 1  77 of them: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'unicorn'   (tests/kernel64_machine.py)   26 of them: StubError, same cause                            (tests/boot64_stubtest.py)```(`logs/07-unittest-BASE-no-unicorn.txt`.) The fix, all of it inside the repository:| Change | Effect, measured ||---|---|| `requirements-harness.txt` — `unicorn==2.1.4` | the pin exists in the tree, so CI and a human install the same version; it is the version `tests/test_release.py` requires `ci/build.yml` to install, and that test still passes || `tests/test_kernel64.py`, `tests/test_boot64_stub.py`: class-level `@unittest.skipUnless(HAVE_UNICORN, …)` | the module-dependent tests **skip with a stated reason** instead of erroring, and the suite total is identical either way (`Ran 925` present or absent), so a skip cannot be mistaken for a pass || `README.md`, `docs/BUILDING.md` | the venv provisioning that `ci/build.yml` performs, with the PEP 668 reason for the venv spelled out: a bare `pip install -r requirements-harness.txt` into a distribution Python is **refused**, which the review's own suggestion would have hit || `tests/test_presentation.py` | its two synthetic trees now link `requirements-harness.txt` too; without this, four tests fail in a clean clone (see "found by running it" below) |The skips are not a way of hiding the gap. Running the two affected modules againstthe same tree both ways:```without the module          with the module (provisioned venv)test_kernel64     12 ok     test_kernel64     71 ok      (+59 actually executing)test_boot64_stub    0 ok     test_boot64_stub  48 ok      (+48 actually executing)                            59 + 48 = 107 = 109 skipped - 2 skipped```That arithmetic is the proof the decorators changed behaviour rather than labels:exactly the tests that were skipped in the unprovisioned run are the ones that run inthe provisioned run, and no test appears or disappears from the total.**The Git-object half: the review was wrong, and here is the measurement.** A clean,full clone of `main` at `56c5852` resolves every citation with **no** pull-requestrefs present:```$ git clone --no-hardlinks --single-branch --branch main https://github.com/SpicyBadran/PC5.git /tmp/f1clone$ git for-each-ref | wc -l3                                    # refs/heads/main, refs/remotes/origin/main, origin/HEAD$ make check...CITATIONS OK: 258 commit reference(s) resolve across 11 documentsRan 889 tests in 77.689sOK (skipped=2)                                                    exit 0```(`logs/06-make-check-BASE-clean-clone.txt`.) `refs/pull/*/head` is not required for thescanned set, because the scanned set is root-level `*_2026-11-16.md` plus`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, and every commit those cite is an ancestor of `main`. Thefailing case the review recorded came from `/home/user/review-negative`, which holds**327 objects** where a full clone holds 1450 (`git count-objects -v`) — a truncatedcheckout, not a clean clone. The condition is real but it is a different one, and itreproduces on demand:```$ git clone --depth 1 --no-hardlinks --single-branch --branch main https://github.com/SpicyBadran/PC5.git /tmp/f7shallow$ python3 tools/commit_citations.py            # the tool as delivered at 56c5852258 dead commit reference(s) across 11 documents                                              exit 1````git cat-file` cannot distinguish "this object does not exist" from "this clone wasnever given it", so a truncated history looks exactly like dead evidence. The fixmakes the tool say which of the two it is looking at:```$ git apply USB-REMEDIATION-v2.patch && make citations-check258 dead commit reference(s) across 11 documentsCITATIONS INCOMPLETE: this clone cannot resolve the references above. That is not a pass,and it is not proof the citations are dead.    history truncated: shallow clone (.git/shallow present; history depth limited by `--depth`).    A commit excluded by the fetch depth is indistinguishable from a missing one here.    repair the checkout, not the documents: `git fetch --unshallow` or re-clone without    --depth/--filter (CI: fetch-depth: 0), then re-run `make citations-check`.    this clone also holds no pull-request refs, so a citation to a PR head …make: *** [Makefile:320: citations-check] Error 1                                              exit 2```(`logs/13-shallow-clone-guidance.txt`.) It still fails. Nothing was converted into apass: silence and absence are reported as failures, with the repair named.`make citation-refs` (new target, `tools/citation_refs.sh`) covers the case the review*intended* — a document citing a commit that only exists on an open PR head — and itis reproducible against the real remote with no manual `git update-ref`:```$ make citation-refs          # in a fresh clone of main, patchedCITATION REFS OK: 34 pull-request head ref(s) fetched from 'origin' (was 0);`make citations-check` can now resolve PR-head citations.                      exit 0```(`logs/12-citation-refs-on-fresh-clone.txt`, and`logs/12b-citation-refs-target-absent-at-BASE.txt` for the negative control that thetarget does not exist at `56c5852`.) The exitcontract of the script: 0 with a count on success, 1 if the fetch fails, 2 with"not a pass" if the clone has no remote to fetch from.**`make check` after both halves, in environments built only from documented steps.**Every row below is a fresh `git clone` of the public repository; nothing was copied inby hand, and the only manual step is the one the README documents.| # | tree | provisioning | command | exit | result ||---|---|---|---|---|---|| A1 | clone of `main`, patch applied | none | `PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 TMPDIR=/tmp make check` | **0** | `Ran 925 … OK (skipped=109)`, `DOCS OK: 58 Markdown files, 34 make targets`, `CITATIONS OK: 258`, `PRESENTATION OK`, `BRAND OK`, kernel `PASSED: 165 checks, 0 failures`, `PSM conformance: 261/261` || A2 | same clone | `python3 -m venv .venv` + `.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements-harness.txt` + `make citation-refs` | `PATH=$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH TMPDIR=/tmp make check` | **0** | `Ran 925 … OK (skipped=2)`, all the same gates green || — | same clone | — | `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_pc5ctl_usb*.py'` | **0** | `Ran 91 … OK` |Transcripts: `logs/14-final-A1-make-check-no-provisioning.txt`,`logs/16-final-A2-make-check-provisioned.txt`, `logs/15-final-usb-suite-91.txt`.`DOCS OK: 58` in a clone versus `89` in the workspace is the untracked Markdown in thisworking tree, not a difference in what is checked.**Prerequisites, stated.** `make check` itself stays network-free: it needs `make`,`gcc`, `binutils`, `python3` and nothing else, and skips the emulator suites when`unicorn` is absent. The two provisioning steps (venv install, `citation-refs`) arethe only commands that reach the network, they are separate targets, and both aredocumented in `README.md` and `docs/BUILDING.md`.**Residual limits, not closed here.** `make citation-refs` cannot repair a truncatedhistory — the message now says so rather than implying it can. `refs/pull/*/head` is aGitHub-specific namespace; the script fails with a named reason elsewhere, it does notdegrade to a pass. `citations-check` scans only root-level dated records plus`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, so citations in `docs/engineering/**` are unverified by thegate — including the ones in this pass's own remediation record, which is why the14 unresolved PR-head references in the *workspace* are not a CI failure at eitherbase. Changing the scan set would re-judge historical documents against rules writtenafter them, so it was not done.---## F-2 — `HASHES.txt` records its own bytes — CLOSED by additive correction**What the review said (low).** The delivered manifest both declares it cannot hashitself and does exactly that, with a value that is stale.Confirmed by measuring the file as delivered, not by quoting the review:```$ grep -n 'usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/HASHES.txt' evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/HASHES.txt33:evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/HASHES.txt 30519 17b2f47b8049fe3b70c5fee2fcd0c8046be682a77a9ed36e13a6d1084203b9a3$ stat -c '%s bytes' evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/HASHES.txt30878 bytes$ sha256sum evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/HASHES.txt37cf9de4482319e901c60cf78a3e2f88ad5647bda149f3ed0a7c22c86607a24d```Line 33 claims `30519 / 17b2f47b…`; the bytes on disk are `30878 / 37cf9de4…` — a359-byte drift, i.e. the manifest grew after its own record was written, which is whata self-record must do. Its header (lines 22–23) states the rule the line 33 recordbreaks: "This file is the last write of the pass, by design: it cannot hash itself".**What was done.** The original manifest is **preserved untouched** — regenerating itwould change its hash and destroy the very record the review is about. The correctionis additive and lives in `CORRECTED-MANIFEST.txt` beside it. The three questions thefinding asks are answered there, restated:1. **What the manifest covers.** 217 records: every file in   `evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/` (22, and `notes=0` from the audit script   confirms none was missed) plus the `recovery/` archives and manifests and the   cross-referenced documents outside that directory — measured in this workspace at   `2026-11-21T17:20:21Z`. Not the repository, and not anything written after that   second. Its own record is the exception that proves the rule: `HASHES.txt` cannot   carry a hash of its finished bytes, so line 33 was always going to be stale the   moment the file was closed.2. **How the manifest is authenticated.** Not by itself. It is authenticated from   outside: `evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v2/HASHES.txt` records   `37cf9de4…` for the delivered `HASHES.txt`, and this document — which is inside   that manifest — is what ties the two together. The chain ends where every such   chain ends: at the commit that carries the tree, and at whatever the author   publishes outside it. Once the PR branch is committed, `git hash-object` of the   blob and the commit's tree id become the external anchor; that is a property of   Git, and no file in the tree can substitute for it.3. **What a reader should trust.** The `<bytes> <sha256>` pairs, which are checkable   (`python3 evidence/usb-descriptor-independent-review-v1/audit_evidence.py` re-verifies   them: 216 of 217, the 1 exception being this self-record). The prose timestamps are   a separate matter — see F-3.**Not done deliberately.** `HASHES.txt` was not edited, its self-record was notdeleted, and `pristine/` was not touched. A correction that rewrites the artifactunder correction is not a correction.---## F-3 — the 38-second gap between archive and manifest — CLOSED by reconciliation**What the review said (low).** `HASHES.txt` dates the v3 continuity archive to`17:16:48Z`; the manifest inside that archive says `generated 2026-11-21T17:17:26Z`.A member cannot be older than the container that holds it, so one of the two is wrong.**Measured.** Three distinct events were being described by one field, and one of thenumbers is mislabelled:| Event | Recorded as | Where it can still be checked ||---|---|---|| the manifest's `date -u` reading, taken **immediately before** archiving | `generated: 2026-11-21T17:17:26Z` | the manifest text itself, and `generated_note`, which states the method || the tar's creation | a member listing inside the archive: `./pc5-usb-descriptor-remediation-20261121-v3-manifest.json` carries mtime `2026-11-21 17:17` | `tar tzvf recovery/…-v3.tar.gz` || each verification run | its own capture time, in `logs/00-CAPTURE-TIME.txt` and the `# captured` line of every transcript | this package |Because the manifest copy *inside* the tar is stamped `17:17` and declares `17:17:26Z`,the tar was built **at or after 17:17:26Z**. The `17:16:48Z` in `HASHES.txt:24-25`,written as "the v3 continuity archive … was built at 17:16:48Z", is therefore not thebuild time: it is a pre-archiving `date -u` reading — the same convention`generated_note` describes — transcribed into a sentence that calls it a build time.The 38-second gap is that mislabelling, not a contradiction between two builds and nota backdating. No timestamp was altered, moved or invented to make this story close;the arithmetic is `17:16:48 < 17:17:26 ≤ tar creation`, and it is reproducible from thearchive as delivered.**Why the mtimes cannot be used to argue about this, recorded now so nobody re-derives it:**```$ stat -c '%n %y' recovery/pc5-usb-descriptor-remediation-20261121-v3.tar.gz recovery/pc5-…-v3-manifest.jsonrecovery/…-v3.tar.gz         2026-11-21 18:04:50.195741291 +0000recovery/…-v3-manifest.json  2026-11-21 18:04:50.195741291 +0000```Identical to the nanosecond, and 48 minutes after both events. This sandbox rewritesmtimes when the workspace is restored, so filesystem times in `recovery/` record therestore, not the writing. Ordering claims must come from inside the archive, as above.**One further defect found while measuring this, disclosed rather than fixed.** The`archive` field of all three remediation manifests names the **manifest**, not thearchive: `v1 → pc5-usb-descriptor-remediation-20261121-v1.json`,`v2 → …-v2.json`, `v3 → …-v3.json`, while the files they document are`.tar.gz`. A tool that reads `archive` to decide what to hash would hash the manifestand report success on the wrong object. The archives are still identifiable: the v3sidecar verifies (`sha256sum -c recovery/…-v3.tar.gz.sha256` →`pc5-usb-descriptor-remediation-20261121-v3.tar.gz: OK`,`ea6329e4e4b8287317d126f59d62f82f3aaa7cd6ca9abd4b73c662fbe700eb41`, 927,493 B,193 members). The manifests are historical records and were not rewritten to tidy afield.---## Corrections to the review's own recordRecording these is not a courtesy to the implementation; each one changes what areader should conclude.1. **F-1's second half is withdrawn as written.** "A clean clone requires   `refs/pull/*/head`" is false for a full clone of `main` at `56c5852`: measured exit   0 with 3 refs and 258/258 citations resolved. The true condition is a truncated   history (shallow or filtered clone), and open-PR-head citations only matter for   documents outside the gate's scan set. The fix covers both, and names them   separately, so the correction stands even though the diagnosis did not.2. **The review's "+5 citation failures" in the unprovisioned run did not come from   the repository.** The base tree without `unicorn` fails `Ran 889 … FAILED   (errors=103, skipped=24)` with **zero** citation failures   (`logs/07-unittest-BASE-no-unicorn.txt`). The 5 failures the review recorded came   from its own tree, which had copied untracked documents into a partial clone.3. **`logs/make-check-base-clone-run1.txt` in the review package is a defective   record and must not be trusted as an exit status.** Its header claims exit 0 over a   body copied from the run that failed because a draft probe file of the reviewer's   sat in that clone's `tests/`. The clean run is `logs/make-check-base-clone.txt`. The   file is left exactly as it was — it is evidence that the reviewer produced a bad   record — and `make-check-base-clone-run1.txt.CORRIGENDUM.txt` beside it says so.4. **F-5 refined, not closed.** The review recorded the prior 8-mutant harness as   returning `RESULT: PROBLEM` with 5 mutants whose named tests did not fail, and read   that as name drift against the *current* suite. Measured three ways with the same   harness and the same production code:   | test inventory | result |   |---|---|   | base `tests/test_pc5ctl_usb_safety.py` only, 56 tests | `RESULT: PROBLEM: [m1, m3, m5, m6, m8]` |   | prior inventory, 87 tests (`logs/19-final-prior-8-mutants-vs-87.txt`) | `RESULT: PASS`, every mutant 1–4 genuine assertion failures |   | current inventory, 91 tests (`logs/20-prior-8-mutants-vs-CURRENT-91.txt`) | `RESULT: PROBLEM: [m1, m3, m5, m6, m8]`, every mutant still producing 1–6 assertion failures |   So the harness is coherent with the inventory it was written against; it is stale   with respect to the current one. Its *detection* is not in doubt — under all three   readings every mutant produced assertion failures — but its *intended-test   mapping* is, which is what F-5 said, and F-5 stays **OPEN** for the same reason:   a `RESULT: PASS` line that checks names should not be quoted as stronger than it   is. The 9-mutant harness of this pass (`logs/17-final-mutation-harness-9.txt`,   `RESULT: PASS: baseline green and every mutation detected by its intended   assertion`) is the authoritative one; its own four insensitive   intended-detector entries are F-5 and remain open.---## Independent verification after the correctionsNot a re-run of the implementation's script against its own log files: each row belowwas executed in a throwaway clone of the public repository with the patch applied, orre-executed and re-hashed here.| What | Command (as run) | Result ||---|---|---|| 91-test USB suite | `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_pc5ctl_usb*.py'` in a clean patched clone | `Ran 91 … OK`, exit 0 || 9-mutant harness | `python3 evidence/usb-descriptor-remediation-v1/mutation_harness.py` in a clean patched clone | `RESULT: PASS: baseline green and every mutation detected by its intended assertion`, exit 0; m1–m9 each caught by a named assertion || prior 87-test suite | the three `tests/test_pc5ctl_usb_*.py` from `recovery/pc5-identity-boundary-20261121-v5.tar.gz` (`1485d2e370a89eb7…`, 435,045 B) copied over a clean patched clone | `Ran 87 … OK`, exit 0 — independently authored tests, this pass's production (`logs/18-final-prior-87-suite.txt`) || prior 8-mutant harness | `python3 evidence/identity-boundary-v1/usb_identity_mutations.py` against the 87-test inventory | `RESULT: PASS`, all 8 detected (`logs/19-final-prior-8-mutants-vs-87.txt`) || full `make check`, unprovisioned | `PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 TMPDIR=/tmp make check` | exit 0, `Ran 925 … OK (skipped=109)` || full `make check`, provisioned | `PATH=$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH TMPDIR=/tmp make check` after the documented venv in